I'd certainly rather see the list of known bugs
(platform specific or not) drop to zero before any
new release. There can't be anything in the world
more annoying than seeing your most hated bug not
get fixed in a bugfix release.

I'd also rather see twin releases: a bugfix release
of 3.4.3, followed the same day by a features
release if one is forthcoming, so that there is a
fall-back debugged version to play if the new
features version turns out to have some really
killer bugs.

An alternative would be to have one release, but
with all of the new features under on/off control of
a single command line parameter, so playing the game
"without the new bugs" would be possible.

The bane of usability of projects the size of say
operating systems or web browsers or NetHack is that
programmers just cannot stand the sheer boredom of
doing debugging, and _always_ twiddle with features
instead, a more amusing way to spend programming
hours.

Microsoft, the easy punching bag example of buggy
software builders, has never released a usable OS,
much less a reliable one, because of the many, many
bugs each new release introduces, and the
profitability of always having the version in use be
one people want to replace "to get rid of bugs".

What happens instead is that the old familiar bugs
that can be worked around are replaced with new,
unfamiliar bugs that cannot until workarounds are
discovered.

Let's don't do that for NetHack_next_release.

